'State of Bihar v. Abdul Majid', (G
), held that the Supreme Court of India was in no way bound by the decisions given by the former Federal Court of India or by the Privy Council. There is no discussion but the conclusion may be sustaned on the basis that, in the view of the Supreme Court, neither the Privy Council nor the Federal Court were Courts of co-ordinate jurisdiction with the Supreme Court. It is, therefore, manifest that the binding nature of the precedents of one Court on another depends upon the fact whether such Courts are Courts of co-ordinate jurisdiction or not. This leads us to a more difficult question, viz., what are Courts of co-ordinate jurisdiciton ?
